=== Introduction
--- Baptist Voices [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School · Voices of the Fundamentalist Movement
heading: Baptist Voices
sub: Why the preachers who shaped Independent Fundamental Baptist churches in the twentieth century said, plainly, that they were Baptists, and glad of it.
lead: A heritage bought with blood is not worn lightly. These men inherited the same conviction traced through every name in our earlier study, and they said so, in their own words, from their own pulpits.

=== Jack Hyles
--- Embrace Tradition
kicker: Jack Hyles
image: lessons/history/baptist-voices-1.jpg | Baptism in a creek, congregation gathered on the bank |
quote: I am a Baptist. I thank God that I am a Baptist. I thank God for my Baptist heritage. I thank God for Baptist distinctives. I thank God for the men who died for them. If they can be martyred for these Baptist distinctives, the least I can do is believe them. | Jack Hyles, “What Is a Fundamental Baptist Church?”

--- American Revival
kicker: Jack Hyles
image: lessons/history/baptist-voices-2.jpg | Crowd seated under a revival tent |
quote: I am a Baptist fundamentalist, so I go back to the faith and practice of Baptist churches as founded by Jesus during His earthly ministry! | Jack Hyles, “Let’s Be Baptists!” in *Enemies of Soul-Winning*

--- Independent Fundamental Baptist
kicker: Jack Hyles
image: lessons/history/baptist-voices-3.jpg | Five men standing together at a church gathering |
quote: I am a Baptist. I have no animosity towards anybody else. There are saved people in many different groups. I have dear friends who are fine Christian people, yet are not members of Baptist churches. But, I do thank God that I am a Baptist, and I love my Baptist heritage. | Jack Hyles, *The Church*

=== John R. Rice
--- Retvrn
kicker: John R. Rice
image: lessons/history/baptist-voices-4.jpg | Baptismal service with a large congregation gathered at the water |
quote: I am here in a Baptist church, and I am a Baptist preacher… | John R. Rice, “The Unvarying God”
p: Rice consistently identified as a Baptist preacher and evangelist while emphasizing biblical authority and soul-winning over denominational loyalty. He broke with the Southern Baptist Convention and founded independent Baptist ministries and *The Sword of the Lord*.

=== Lester Roloff
--- Nothing but the Book
kicker: Lester Roloff
image: lessons/history/baptist-voices-5.jpg | Preacher at a pulpit holding up a Bible |
quote: And as far as a lot of Baptists are concerned, I guess I’m just a knothole Baptist; I’m on the outside but still seeing and saying what the Lord wants me to say which, when summed up, makes me a premillennial Southern Baptist, especially since I live in Corpus Christi with love for all and malice toward none. | Lester Roloff, 1956 “Swan Song” address at Baylor University
p: In the same message, Roloff called for a return to “the old landmarks, namely the sovereignty of the local church, the independence of the pulpit, the authority of the Word of God…”

=== Closing Thought
--- Heritage, Held Plainly
lead: None of these men were ashamed of the name. They stood in a line stretching back through every century of this study, and they said so from the pulpit, not out of pride in a denomination, but out of loyalty to a doctrine of the church older than any denomination’s name.
take: Nothing But the Book
